nca Muere! Promising Factors Contributing to the Reclassification of Former Long-Term English Learners in One Urban High School\n\nDescription:\nThe Long-term English learner population is rapidly growing across the United States. Long Term English Learners (LTELs). California Education Code (CDE) defines LTELs as students who have been enrolled in a US school for more than six years and have not acquired the required level of English proficiency. Many of these students have been enrolled in a US school since kindergarten. LTELs participate in educational programs framed to develop their English language acquisition while trying to master academic content. In California, the high number of LTELs in high school poses a challenge to Local Educational Agencies (LEA) in trying to identify the best way to educate this student population. The needs of LTELs are different from newcomers and their native English speaking peers. Despite the many challenges LTELs face when trying to attain English language proficiency and the challenges that schools face in looking into the systems they have in place to provide effective programs for LTELs, there are some LTELs that have met the reclassification criteria and exiting the LTEL status in high school. This study identified the factors that supported three former LTELs who reclassified in one urban high school in southern California.\n\nEvaluate whether this book satisfies the user's search intent.\n\nReturn:\n\n* score (0, 1, or 2)\n* concise reason\n", 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'gpt-4o-mini', 'response_format': {'type': 'json_schema', 'json_schema': {'schema': {'description': 'Structured relevance judgment for a single (query, book) pair.\n\nThe ``reason`` field is mandatory so every judgment can be audited\nor used for fine-tuning the judge model later.', 'properties': {'score': {'description': 'Relevance score: 0 = not relevant, 1 = somewhat relevant, 2 = highly relevant.', 'title': 'Score', 'type': 'integer'}, 'reason': {'de